---
import CommunityLayout from '../components/CommunityLayout.astro';
import { SITE_UPDATED } from '../lib/seo';
---

<CommunityLayout
  title="How iDuckIMG works: what runs in your browser"
  description="How iDuckIMG processes images on your own device: the engines it uses, what is sent over the network, and how you can check it yourself."
  before="How"
  mark="iDuckIMG works"
  intro="Every image tool runs on your device. Here is what that means, which engines do the work and how to check it."
>
  <section class="card p-5" aria-labelledby="short">
    <h2 id="short" class="text-2xl font-black tracking-tight">The short answer</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
      When you choose a picture, your browser reads it from your device and a small program running in a background worker
      changes it there. The result is made in your browser's memory and you save it from there. No picture is sent to our
      servers, because our servers only hand out the page and its code. Last reviewed {SITE_UPDATED}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card p-5" aria-labelledby="engines">
    <h2 id="engines" class="text-2xl font-black tracking-tight">Which engines do the work</h2>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed">
      <li><strong>jSquash</strong> (the codecs from Google's Squoosh, compiled to WebAssembly): reads and writes JPG, PNG and WebP, shrinks PNG files with oxipng, and resizes with high-quality filters.</li>
      <li><strong>libheif</strong> (compiled to WebAssembly): opens iPhone HEIC and HEIF photos. It is only downloaded when you pick a HEIC file.</li>
      <li><strong>pdf-lib</strong> (the maintained @cantoo fork): builds the PDF when you turn pictures into one PDF.</li>
      <li><strong>JSZip</strong>: packs many results into one ZIP file.</li>
      <li><strong>Your browser's own canvas</strong>: rotates, flips, crops, draws text and watermarks, adjusts colors and draws SVG, BMP, GIF and AVIF pictures.</li>
      <li>Metadata removal (EXIF, GPS, camera details, thumbnails) works directly on the file's bytes, so a JPG is not recompressed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      All of this is open-source code that ships with the site. Nothing is loaded from another company's server, with one
      exception: the feedback form uses Cloudflare Turnstile to keep spam out, and only on that page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card p-5" aria-labelledby="check">
    <h2 id="check" class="text-2xl font-black tracking-tight">How to check it yourself</h2>
    <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed">
      <li>Open a tool and press F12 (or open the browser's developer tools).</li>
      <li>Choose the Network tab and clear it.</li>
      <li>Add a picture and run the tool.</li>
      <li>You will see no request carrying your picture. At most you will see our own code files loading, such as the codec for the format you chose.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      The site also tells the browser to refuse anything else: its Content-Security-Policy only allows scripts and files
      from iduckimg.com, so a page cannot quietly send your picture to a third party.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card p-5" aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits" class="text-2xl font-black tracking-tight">Limits you should know about</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
      Because your own device does the work, speed depends on it. Pictures over 25 megapixels show a warning, and pictures
      over 100 megapixels are refused with a message so the tab does not freeze. There is no file-count limit, but files
      are processed one after another. Close the tab when you are done and nothing is kept.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card p-5" aria-labelledby="who">
    <h2 id="who" class="text-2xl font-black tracking-tight">Who is behind it</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed">
      iDuckIMG is made by Basic App Company. Read <a class="link" href="/about/">About</a>, the
      <a class="link" href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> or <a class="link" href="/guides/are-online-image-tools-safe/">how to tell if an online image tool is safe</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</CommunityLayout>
